## Registry (`_registry.py`)
Every detector registers its slug → class mapping here. `Runner` looks up detectors by slug. Never instantiate a detector by class name in production code.

## Algorithm groups and methods

### Distribution diagnostics
Shapiro-Wilk, Anderson-Darling, Lilliefors, KS 1-sample (normality), Hartigan's dip, skewness, kurtosis, ADF, KPSS, Ljung-Box.

### Univariate outliers
Z-score, modified Z-score (MAD), MAD, double MAD, IQR/Tukey fences, adjusted boxplot (medcouple), Grubbs', generalised ESD (Rosner), quantile-based.

**Statistical correctness**: raw Z-score is wrong for heavy-tailed data. Default to modified Z-score (MAD-based) or adjusted boxplot. Only use raw Z-score when the caller explicitly requests it and the data is verified Gaussian.

### Multivariate outliers
Mahalanobis (with MCD for robust covariance), Isolation Forest, LOF, DBSCAN, One-Class SVM, HBOS, COPOD, **ECOD** (default for high-dim tabular), ABOD, PCA reconstruction error, Random Cut Forest, GMM+EM. Autoencoder behind `dqt[deep]`.

### Time series anomalies
STL, Holt-Winters, Prophet (`dqt[forecast]`), seasonal hybrid ESD, Matrix Profile (STUMPY), CUSUM, Page-Hinkley, BOCPD.

### Drift
PSI, KL divergence, Jensen-Shannon, Wasserstein-1 (earth-mover), MMD, KS 2-sample, chi-square (categorical), ADWIN, DDM/EDDM.

### Causal (`dqt[causal]`)
Granger, Transfer Entropy, CCM, PCMCI/PCMCI+, PC, FCI, GES, NOTEARS/DAGMA, LiNGAM, do-calculus (DoWhy), E-value / Rosenbaum sensitivity.

### Information theory & associations
Cramér's V, Theil's U, mutual information.

### Pattern & specialised
Benford's Law, SHAP attribution (`dqt[explain]`), Bayesian networks (pgmpy), functional dependency mining (Tane).

### Dimensionality reduction
PCA, robust PCA.

### Ensembling
Score normalization, average / max / AOM / MOA combinations, stacking, diversity measures.

### Calibration
Empirical-CDF calibration, Platt scaling, isotonic regression, bootstrap CIs.

## Required tests for each detector
1. Known-answer test against a textbook example.
2. Behaviour test on synthetic distributions (drift / no-drift).
3. `hypothesis` property test for numerical stability.
4. Golden-file test for `STAT_SCALE` verdict.
5. (For time-series detectors) seasonality and trend robustness test.

## File location
`packages/dqt/src/dqt/algorithms/<group>/<method>.py`

Canonical paper/reference linked at the top of each file in a single comment line.
